  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nOvernight into Early This Morning: Rain Continues\n\nA cold front is slowly pushing SE through the region tonight \nwith 06Z position of the front from roughly Cedar Rapids, IA \nnortheast to Wisconsin Rapids, WI. A stout ribbon of surface to \n600-mb frontogenesis is the primary driver for the precipitation\nthat is ongoing in the immediate wake of the surface front, \nwhich will continue through early in the morning as the front \nsags to the southeast. Climatologically anomalous moisture off \nthe western Gulf coupled with this forcing will lead to \nefficient rainfall rates. The heaviest band of rainfall to occur\nthus far fell along a narrow strip from Dodge County, MN \nnortheast to Pepin County, WI where radar estimates and gauges \nwere reporting between 1.5 and 2 inches.\n\nThis frontogenesis is progged to undergo intensification right \nabout now (likely responsible for the increase in radar echoes\nin the last 1-2 hours) with the strengthening low-level jet \nbefore weakening after sunrise, resulting in these higher rain \nrates continuing through the night as the front slides into \nwestern and central Wisconsin. The general consensus is that \nmost locales will see between 1/4\" and 3/4\", with several \nadditional bands of 1-2\" likely before sunrise.\n\n\nThe Upcoming Week: Seasonable and Mostly Dry\n\nFollowing the cold frontal passage overnight, highs for today\nwill fall 15 degrees compared to yesterday with most locales\ntopping out in the low to mid 40s under a brisk northwest wind\ngusting to 25 to 30 mph. Skies clear out for most areas this \nafternoon as surface ridging builds in from the west, but a \nlobe of moisture and lift attendant with a mid-level shortwave \npassage could result in flurries north of I-94 after sunset and \ninto Monday morning. Continued strong cold air advection drives \nhighs down into the 30s for Monday with only a slow recovery to \nthe low 40s for highs by the end of the week. Lows during this \ntime fall into the teens and 20s with single digits plausible \n(20-30%) Monday and Tuesday nights.\n\nThe region resides in a split flow pattern for the start of the\nweek, keeping the threat of precipitation at bay. A disjointed system\nis progged to graze the region Wednesday into Thursday, possibly\nbringing some light snow to the region. Given the variability in\nhow the northern and southern stream waves will interact with\none another, there is naturally higher variability with how this\nmid to late week system will play out. There are a few outlier\nensemble models (10-30%) that have over 1 inch of snow with the\npassage of this system, mainly towards southern Wisconsin.",
